---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import LoremIpsumGenerator from '@/components/tools/text/LoremIpsumGenerator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="lorem-ipsum-generator">
  <LoremIpsumGenerator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to generate placeholder text</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Choose what you need: paragraphs, sentences, individual words or list items.</li>
      <li>Set the amount with the slider or type an exact figure.</li>
      <li>Pick a style. Classic Latin is traditional. The modern styles help when a client asks what the Latin means.</li>
      <li>Turn on HTML wrapping if the text goes into a template, and set the paragraph length to match your real content.</li>
      <li>Copy the result, download it, or press <strong>Generate new text</strong> for different text.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Where lorem ipsum comes from</h2>
    <p>
      The lorem ipsum passage is corrupted Latin from <em>De Finibus Bonorum et Malorum</em>, a
      treatise on ethics that Cicero wrote in 45 BC. The opening words come from section 1.10.32:
      <em>“Neque porro quisquam est qui dolorem ipsum quia dolor sit amet…”</em> (“Nor is there anyone
      who loves pain itself because it is pain.”). A Renaissance typesetter scrambled it into a
      specimen sheet, and the printing industry has used it since. Letraset put it on dry-transfer
      sheets in the 1960s, and desktop publishing software brought it into the digital era.
    </p>
    <p>
      It is still used because its word lengths and letter frequencies are like those of English and
      other Latin-alphabet languages. So a block of it has the right visual texture, but it has no
      meaning, and nobody edits the copy during a layout review.
    </p>

    <h2>Choosing a style</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Style</th>
            <th>Best for</th>
            <th>Watch out for</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Classic Latin</td>
            <td>Print layouts, type specimens, anything a designer will review</td>
            <td>Clients occasionally think the site is broken or in the wrong language</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Plain English</td>
            <td>Testing readability and line-breaking with real word shapes</td>
            <td>People start reading and commenting on the words</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Tech &amp; startup</td>
            <td>SaaS mock-ups, pitch decks, product pages</td>
            <td>Can be mistaken for approved marketing copy</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Corporate jargon</td>
            <td>Slide decks and annual-report layouts</td>
            <td>Same risk. Label it clearly as placeholder</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Hipster</td>
            <td>Demos where you want it to be obvious the copy is fake</td>
            <td>Not appropriate for a formal client presentation</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Recipe &amp; food</td>
            <td>Food blogs, menus, editorial layouts</td>
            <td>Slightly longer average word length than Latin</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>How much text do you actually need?</h2>
    <p>
      Match the placeholder to the real content, or the layout gives a wrong impression. A hero
      paragraph is usually 20–40 words, a product description 40–70, a blog paragraph 50–80, and a full
      article section 150–300. If the finished page will have three-sentence paragraphs, ten-sentence
      ones make the design look much denser than it will be. Set the sentences-per-paragraph control to
      what you expect.
    </p>

    <h2>Accessibility and SEO warnings</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Never publish it. A page indexed with lorem ipsum can be classed as thin or low-quality content, and real visitors lose trust when they see it.</li>
      <li>Do not use it in alt text. Screen-reader users get nothing from “lorem ipsum dolor”. For decorative images, leave the attribute empty.</li>
      <li>Do not use it in email templates you send. Filler in a live send is a classic mistake.</li>
      <li>Search for it before launch. A search for “lorem” across your templates and CMS finds almost all stray blocks.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Everything is generated on your device</h2>
    <p>
      The word banks and the lorem ipsum generator are part of the page, so pressing generate makes no
      request. It works offline, makes unlimited text, and adds no “powered by” line to what you copy.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
